Do not abort() when a shader fails to build.

A libretro core runs in-process with its frontend, so abort() does not
just end the emulator -- it takes the frontend down with it.  On
Adreno/freedreno that is not hypothetical: the VDP2 blit shader wants up
to 18 fragment texture samplers and the driver offers 16, so the link
fails and REG-Station dies with the core.

Treat a shader that will not build as a reason to draw nothing:

  - compileVDP2Prog() returns int instead of aborting.  The failing
    helper already clears _prgid[id], so the caller binds program 0 --
    the frame is wrong, the emulator survives.
  - setupVDP2Prog() and YglInitDrawFrameBufferShaders() propagate that
    failure; Ygl_uniformVDP2DrawFramebuffer() gains the pgid < 0 guard it
    needs now that setupVDP2Prog() can fail (it indexed _prgid[pgid]
    unconditionally).
  - initVDPProg() is void, so it logs, clears the program handle and
    returns rather than aborting.
  - The compile/link aborts in YglBlitSimple(), YglBlitVDP1() and
    YglUpscaleFramebuffer() become "return -1"; all three already return
    int.

Also fixes an out-of-bounds read in YglInitDrawFrameBufferShaders(): it
called compileVDP2Prog() -- which indexes pYglprg_vdp2_blit_f[arrayid] --
*before* range-checking arrayid.  The check now runs first.

The aborts left alone are in commongl.c: framebuffer-completeness checks,
an allocation failure and a NULL Vdp2 regs guard.  Those are not shader
build failures and giving each caller an error path is a separate job.

